<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  V2-17: the network policy is now declared, not inherited.

  Nothing this app sends is optional to protect: message bodies, contact
  names, and the encrypted Drive backup all leave the device from this
  process. The policy that keeps them on TLS was, until now, entirely
  implicit — targetSdk 28+ happens to default `cleartextTrafficPermitted` to
  false, and API 24+ happens to stop trusting user-installed CAs. Both are
  defaults. A future targetSdk change, a manifest merge from a library, or a
  `usesCleartextTraffic="true"` added to fix one endpoint would move them
  without anyone deciding to, and nothing in the tree would have recorded
  what the intended posture was.

  This file records it. `NetworkSecurityConfigTest` holds it in place.

  WHAT THIS DOES AND DOES NOT COVER

  In-process traffic is the whole of it:
    - `SafeHttp` — link previews, which fetch URLs chosen by whoever sent the
      message. Already HTTPS-only and port-443-only by validation; this is the
      second lock on that door.
    - `DriveClient` — hardcoded https://www.googleapis.com endpoints.

  MMS is *not* affected and must not be assumed to be. Send and receive go
  through `SmsManager.sendMultimediaMessage` / `downloadMultimediaMessage`,
  so the carrier MMSC exchange — which is very often plain http:// — happens
  in the system telephony process under its own policy. Denying cleartext
  here does not break MMS, and would not secure it either.

  Google sign-in likewise runs in the Play Services process, not this one.

  TRUST ANCHORS: SYSTEM ONLY

  Omitting `<certificates src="user" />` refuses CAs a person installed by
  hand, which is how TLS interception is set up. That is the point: a
  messaging app is exactly the target worth intercepting. Enterprise CAs
  delivered by device policy land in the *system* store, so managed devices
  are unaffected — what this refuses is manual interception.

  The debug build overlays this file (`src/debug/res/xml/`) to add the user
  store back, so a proxy still works when you are debugging. It changes
  nothing else, deliberately: debug and release should behave identically on
  the wire, because a difference there produces bugs that only appear in
  release.

  CERTIFICATE PINNING: DELIBERATELY ABSENT

  Pinning googleapis.com would trade a small attack-surface reduction for a
  real availability risk. Google rotates intermediates on their schedule, not
  ours; a pin that expires while an app is in the field bricks backup and
  restore for every install until a store update propagates, and there is no
  remote kill switch here to undo it. The endpoints are Google-operated and
  already covered by Certificate Transparency. If pinning is ever added it
  needs a backup pin and a tested rotation plan first.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
